The sorted order lets the search finish in logarithmic time. The binary search looks for the first number greater than or equal to `x`. That number is the ceil. The value just before it is the floor.

If the middle value is smaller than `x`, it can be the floor, so the left side is moved forward. Otherwise, it may be the ceil, so the right side moves back.

```java
class Solution {
    public int[] getFloorAndCeil(int[] nums, int x) {
        int left = 0;
        int right = nums.length;

        while (left < right) {
            int middle = left + (right - left) / 2;
            if (nums[middle] < x) {
                left = middle + 1;
            } else {
                right = middle;
            }
        }

        int ceilValue = left < nums.length ? nums[left] : -1;
        int floorValue = left > 0 && nums[left - 1] <= x ? nums[left - 1] : -1;

        if (left < nums.length && nums[left] == x) {
            floorValue = x;
        }

        return new int[] {floorValue, ceilValue};
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(log n)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)`.

Dry run for `[2, 5, 9]` and `x = 6`: the first value not below `6` is `9`, so the floor is the previous value, `5`, and the ceil is `9`.

Instead of trying to get both answers from one position, this code performs two small binary searches. The first finds the last value less than or equal to `x`. The second finds the first value greater than or equal to `x`.

This makes the two conditions very clear. If either search runs out of the array, its answer stays `-1`. The input array is only read.

```csharp
public class Solution {
    public int[] GetFloorAndCeil(int[] nums, int x) {
        int floorIndex = FindLastAtMost(nums, x);
        int ceilIndex = FindFirstAtLeast(nums, x);

        int floorValue = floorIndex == -1 ? -1 : nums[floorIndex];
        int ceilValue = ceilIndex == nums.Length ? -1 : nums[ceilIndex];

        return new int[] { floorValue, ceilValue };
    }

    private int FindLastAtMost(int[] nums, int x) {
        int left = 0;
        int right = nums.Length - 1;
        int answer = -1;

        while (left <= right) {
            int middle = left + (right - left) / 2;
            if (nums[middle] <= x) {
                answer = middle;
                left = middle + 1;
            } else {
                right = middle - 1;
            }
        }

        return answer;
    }

    private int FindFirstAtLeast(int[] nums, int x) {
        int left = 0;
        int right = nums.Length;

        while (left < right) {
            int middle = left + (right - left) / 2;
            if (nums[middle] < x) {
                left = middle + 1;
            } else {
                right = middle;
            }
        }

        return left;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(log n)`, since there are two binary searches.
Space complexity: `O(1)`.

The important edge cases are an empty array, `x` below the first value, and `x` above the last value. In those cases one or both returned values are `-1`.

A simple pass is enough here. While reading the sorted values, keep the largest value below or equal to `x` as the floor and stop at the first value that can be the ceil.

For `nums = [1, 4, 6, 8]` and `x = 5`, the scan keeps `4` as the floor and finds `6` as the ceil. The code returns `-1` when one of them does not exist. It also leaves the input unchanged.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> getFloorAndCeil(vector<int> nums, int x) {
        int floorValue = -1;
        int ceilValue = -1;

        for (int value : nums) {
            if (value <= x) {
                floorValue = value;
            } else {
                ceilValue = value;
                break;
            }
        }

        return {floorValue, ceilValue};
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n)` in the worst case.
Space complexity: `O(1)` apart from the returned array.

An empty array, or a value smaller than every element, is handled by the initial `-1` values.

This version finds the first value that is not smaller than `x` with a recursive search. The returned index separates the two answers: the item at the index is the ceil, and the item immediately before it is the floor.

The helper uses a half-open range, so an empty range is easy to handle. No values are changed, and missing bounds remain `-1`.

```go
func getFloorAndCeil(nums []int, x int) []int {
	position := firstAtLeast(nums, x, 0, len(nums))

	floorValue := -1
	ceilValue := -1

	if position < len(nums) {
		ceilValue = nums[position]
	}
	if position > 0 {
		floorValue = nums[position-1]
	}

	return []int{floorValue, ceilValue}
}

func firstAtLeast(nums []int, x int, left int, right int) int {
	if left == right {
		return left
	}

	middle := left + (right-left)/2
	if nums[middle] < x {
		return firstAtLeast(nums, x, middle+1, right)
	}
	return firstAtLeast(nums, x, left, middle)
}
```

Time complexity: `O(log n)`.
Space complexity: `O(log n)` because of the recursive calls.

Dry run with `[1, 4, 6, 10]` and `x = 6` ends at index `2`. Both answers are `6`, since the value is present.

## Solution
```java[]
class Solution {
    public int[] getFloorAndCeil(int[] nums, int x) {

        int floor = -1;
        int ceil = -1;

        for(int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {

            if(nums[i] == x) {
                return new int[]{x, x};
            }

            if(nums[i] < x) {
                floor = nums[i];
            }

            if(nums[i] > x) {
                ceil = nums[i];
                break;
            }
        }

        return new int[]{floor, ceil};
    }
}
```

## Approach
Perform two binary searches on the sorted array: the first finds the largest element less than or equal to x (floor), and the second finds the smallest element greater than or equal to x (ceil).

Return the floor and ceil values found; if either does not exist, return -1 for that value.

## Solution
```cpp[]
class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> getFloorAndCeil(vector<int> nums, int x) {

        int first = 0;
        int last = nums.size() - 1;

        int floor = -1;
        int ceil = -1;

        // Floor
        while(first <= last){
            int mid = first + (last - first)/2;
            if(nums[mid] <= x){
                floor = nums[mid];
                first = mid + 1;
            }
            else{
                last = mid - 1;
            }
        }

        first = 0;
        last = nums.size() - 1;

        // Ceil
        while(first <= last){
            int mid = first + (last - first)/2;
            if(nums[mid] >= x){
                ceil = nums[mid];
                last = mid - 1;
            }
            else{
                first = mid + 1;
            }
        }
        return {floor, ceil};
    }
};
```

